WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.370
Doctor Sleep is apparently a cinematic awakening.

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:07.790
After attending an exclusive screening for
the latest Stephen King adaptation ahead of

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:12.150
its November release, critics have flooded
Twitter with their thoughts on Doctor Sleep.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:15.000
So far, reactions are extraordinarily positive.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.999
A sequel to King's seminal novel The Shining,
which was itself adapted into a classic film

00:00:19.999 --> 00:00:25.300
by Stanley Kubrick in 1980, director Mike
Flanagan's Doctor Sleep follows an adult Danny

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.359
Torrance, played by Ewan McGregor.

00:00:27.359 --> 00:00:31.789
The grown up Danny is revealed to still be
haunted by his childhood ability known as

00:00:31.789 --> 00:00:35.200
the "shining," which induces visions and other
psychic happenings.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.340
Though he desperately wants to live a normal
life and establish some sort of peace for

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:43.690
himself, Danny is still traumatized by what
happened to him as a child at The Shining's

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:45.630
haunted Overlook Hotel.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:49.860
Things only get more complicated when he meets
children who share his special gift, namely

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:55.320
a girl named Abra, who asks for Danny's protection
against cult leader Rose the Hat, and her

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:56.320
underlings.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.010
His fears have never been greater, but Danny
knows he must do everything in his power to

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:04.180
protect Abra and other children with the "shine"
from forces of evil.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:08.710
"I only met two or three people like us.

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:11.590
They died."

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:15.450
Before we get to how the critics feel about
Doctor Sleep, we should reveal what the most

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:17.319
important reviewer has to say.

00:01:17.319 --> 00:01:22.310
Stephen King himself took to Twitter to share
his unfiltered feelings toward Doctor Sleep

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:24.860
and his reaction was totally positive.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:30.030
Praising the big-screen adaptation of his
2013 novel, King called Doctor Sleep an immersive

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:33.999
experience, and commended director Flanagan
for his storytelling skills.

00:01:33.999 --> 00:01:34.999
King tweeted:

00:01:34.999 --> 00:01:40.180
"DOCTOR SLEEP: Mike Flanagan is a talented
director, but he's also an excellent storyteller.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:41.799
The movie is a good thing.

00:01:41.799 --> 00:01:45.670
You'll like this if you liked THE SHINING,
but you'll also like it if you liked SHAWSHANK."

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:49.979
King is no stranger to adaptations of his
work, but this is high praise coming from

00:01:49.979 --> 00:01:53.689
the author, who famously disliked Kubrick's
take on The Shining.

00:01:53.689 --> 00:01:58.529
In fact, King infamously compared the film
which he called "cold" to a, quote, "big,

00:01:58.529 --> 00:02:01.369
beautiful Cadillac with no engine inside it."

00:02:01.369 --> 00:02:05.350
Now, onto the critics who have no personal
stake in the worlds of The Shining and its

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:06.850
successor Doctor Sleep.

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:10.879
The Daily Dead News' Heather Wixson had nothing
but praise for Doctor Sleep, tweeting:

00:02:10.879 --> 00:02:15.280
"It's been a great [year] for Stephen King
adaptations but I think DoctorSleep is the

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:16.540
best of the bunch.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:21.689
Thrilled me in ways I wasn't expecting, made
me emotional at times &amp; Rebecca Ferguson is

00:02:21.689 --> 00:02:22.689
ferocious.

00:02:22.689 --> 00:02:26.890
What a wonderful celebration of Kubrick, King
&amp; [Flanagan's] career as well."

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:32.400
CinemaBlend's senior film critic Eric Eisenberg
was also all in on Doctor Sleep which is saying

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:37.700
something considering he's a self-proclaimed
Shining fanatic, King diehard, and Mike Flanagan

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:38.700
enthusiast.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:39.700
Eisenberg tweeted:

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:44.689
"As a huge fan of Kubrick's The Shining, King's
books &amp; Mike Flanagan, I'm giddy that Doctor

00:02:44.689 --> 00:02:46.330
Sleep is seriously GREAT.

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:51.670
It's an excellent &amp; faithful adaptation that
makes smart changes especially melding the

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:56.019
'80 film &amp; the sequel book and the casting/performances
are phenomenal."

00:02:56.019 --> 00:02:59.630
Jacob Hall, managing editor at Slashfilm,
also adored the movie.

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:00.989
He wrote in a tweet:

00:03:00.989 --> 00:03:05.670
"DOCTOR SLEEP: The boldest choice here is
that Mike Flanagan doesn't even try to do

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:06.670
Kubrick.

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:10.920
He makes a Mike Flanagan movie: spooky, emotional,
ultimately optimistic.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:16.430
Not only a sequel to THE SHINING, but a response
to it, an attempt to reconcile King and Kubrick.

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:17.430
Loved it."

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:21.860
The performances in Doctor Sleep are also
highlighted by critics, including ComicBook.com's

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:23.189
Brandon Davis.

00:03:23.189 --> 00:03:24.189
He tweeted:

00:03:24.189 --> 00:03:26.989
"Fans of TheShining are really gonna love
DoctorSleep.

00:03:26.989 --> 00:03:29.299
It's at its best when [it's] fueled by follow-up.

00:03:29.299 --> 00:03:32.959
Starts slowly and descends into madness, just
like its predecessor.

00:03:32.959 --> 00:03:37.970
One specific scene is really horrifying, some
solid scares, and good performances across

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:38.970
the board."

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:42.909
Though the vast majority of critics can't
say enough good things about Doctor Sleep,

00:03:42.909 --> 00:03:45.260
one didn't feel completely blown away by it.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:50.359
Yes, I Do Love Movies! creator John Duarte
admitted in a tweet that he was a bit underwhelmed

00:03:50.359 --> 00:03:51.359
by the film.

00:03:51.359 --> 00:03:52.359
He tweeted:

00:03:52.359 --> 00:03:56.980
"DoctorSleep is a good movie but didn't WOW
me like writer/director Mike Flanagan's previous

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:57.980
work."

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:01.981
Overall, though, it looks like fans of Stephen
King's novel and Stanley Kubrick's The Shining

00:04:01.981 --> 00:04:07.090
need not worry: Flanagan, his cast, and his
crew have apparently created a sequel that

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:11.489
does justice to both its source material and
its cinematic predecessor.

00:04:11.489 --> 00:04:14.920
Doctor Sleep arrives in theaters on November
8.

